  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n\nSurface high pressure shifts east this weekend which will allow \nwarmer temperatures and higher dew points to move back into the area \nfrom the south. Shower and thunderstorm chances return with the \nsummer-like conditions as northwesterly flow aloft persists. Rain \nchances remain lower during the day on Sunday with lack of forcing \nbefore increased rain chances develop Sunday night and into early \nnext week. One potential period for increased storm activity is \nMonday evening and Monday night. Instability may end up overlapping \nwith strong shear (by July standards) when a potential cluster of \nstorms or MCS sinks into the Ohio Valley from the northwest. Details \nremain very murky without CAM guidance to resolve potential \nconvection, but there are descent probabilities for storms somewhere \nin the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley late Monday. Additionally, storm \nchances on Sunday night cannot be ruled out with favorable shear and \ninstability in place and some guidance showing at least some storm \npotential in the region. \n\nAdditionally, the heat index may reach 95-100 degrees on Monday \nafternoon.",
